\section{Which function should I use?}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Approximate full-model-coordinate inference.} Use
\code{\link[=coxlasso_debias]{coxlasso_debias()}} for a ridge-stabilized one-step correction.
Optionally use \code{\link[=coxlasso_boot]{coxlasso_boot()}} with
\code{inference = "debiased"} and either \code{type = "wild"}
or \code{type = "efron"} for a score-level bootstrap.

This is an approximate, research-stage method. Neither the
one-step correction nor the bootstrap alone establishes
general high-dimensional confidence-interval validity.
\item \strong{Fixed-support model-based bootstrap.} Use \code{\link[=coxlasso_boot]{coxlasso_boot()}} with
\code{inference = "conditional"}. The default bootstrap type is
\code{"model_based"}. The original selected support is held fixed
throughout bootstrap refitting.

In this interface, "conditional" means that the \emph{observed}
support is kept fixed. No conditioning on the complete
selection event is performed.
\item \strong{Procedure-level re-selection.}
Use \code{\link[=coxlasso_boot_reselect]{coxlasso_boot_reselect()}} to compare fixed-support refitting
with repeated Cox-Lasso selection. Available workflows include:
\itemize{
\item \code{"fixed_support"}: retain the original support;
\item \code{"reselect_fixed_lambda"}: reselect with a fixed penalty;
\item \code{"reselect_cv_min"}: reselect using CV-min;
\item \code{"reselect_cv_1se"}: reselect using the one-SE rule;
\item \code{"reselect_aic"}: reselect by AIC.
}

The reported bootstrap vectors assign zero to unselected
coordinates; failures remain missing. Selection frequencies
and model overlap describe stability. Resulting intervals
need not have standard fixed-parameter coverage interpretations.
}
